{"text": " You wake up in 1800 near Windsor in England. An astronomer, William Herschel, has set a prism in a sunbeam with thermometers whose bulbs are blackened. Did you know that the hottest spot was not in the rainbow at all, but just past the red where nothing was visible? He had found infrared. The sun was sending more than our eyes could catch. Within a few years, gas lamps will push back the night. You no longer have to stop when the sun goes down. In 1839, in Paris, a 19-year-old named Edmund Beckerl shines light on a cell and it makes electricity. And in 1859, Richard Carrington sees a white flash on the sun. Within a day, telegraph wires spark and operators get shocks. The sun can reach down and touch our machines. It would take another century to turn that tiny spark into power.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 2.22, "text": " You wake up in 1800 near Windsor in England.", "tokens": [50364, 509, 6634, 493, 294, 24327, 2651, 43082, 284, 294, 8196, 13, 50502], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13757637674494308, "compression_ratio": 1.5395189003436427, "no_speech_prob": 0.008848425932228565, "words": [{"word": " You", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.12, "probability": 0.7172796130180359}, {"word": " wake", "start": 0.12, "end": 0.28, "probability": 0.9810481071472168}, {"word": " up", "start": 0.28, "end": 0.46, "probability": 0.9991718530654907}, {"word": " in", "start": 0.46, "end": 0.58, "probability": 0.9418959021568298}, {"word": " 1800", "start": 0.58, "end": 0.88, "probability": 0.9035448431968689}, {"word": " near", "start": 0.88, "end": 1.46, "probability": 0.9495256543159485}, {"word": " Windsor", "start": 1.46, "end": 1.86, "probability": 0.9910184741020203}, {"word": " in", "start": 1.86, "end": 2.0, "probability": 0.6130024194717407}, {"word": " England.", "start": 2.0, "end": 2.22, "probability": 0.9969519376754761}]}, {"id": 1, "seek": 0, "start": 2.8, "end": 4.2, "text": " An astronomer, William Herschel,", "tokens": [5050